          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em cut rule
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"}> formal logic
            ) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rule"} structural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rule"} inference rule
            that from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequents"} sequents
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma \vdash A , \Delta
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Pi, A \vdash \Lambda
          %p the new sequent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Λ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Pi, \Gamma \vdash \Lambda, \Delta
          %p
            may be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction"} deduced
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Π
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Λ
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Λ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi cut
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A, \Delta \quad \Pi, A \vdash \Lambda}{\Pi, \Gamma \vdash \Lambda, \Delta} \; cut.
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            where each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
            here is interpreted as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of morphisms.
          %h2#cut_elimination Cut elimination
          %blockquote
            %p
              “A sequent calculus without cut-elimination is like a car without an engine” – Jean-Yves Girard (in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://iml.univ-mrs.fr/~girard/Synsem.pdf.gz"} Linear logic
          %p
            The
            %em cut-elimination theorem
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"}> Gerhard Gentzen
            ‘s
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gentzen%27s+Hauptsatz"} Hauptsatz
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"} judgement
            which has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            using the cut-rule also has a proof not using it (a “cut-free proof”). While Gentzen's original theorem was for a few particular sequent calculi that he was considering, it is true of many other sequent calculi and is generally seen as desirable. (That said, there are some useful sequent calculi in which it fails.)
          %p
            Intuitively, the problem in deciding whether a formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            follows from a formula
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \vdash B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \vdash C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \vdash B
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is potentially a complicated or large formula. For an automated theorem prover, the search space for such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is potentially infinite. By establishing a cut-elimination theorem for formal systems, one circumvents this problem, and it is quite typical that cut-free proofs build up complex sequents from less complex sequents (cf.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subformula+property"} subformula property
          %p
            Cut-elimination is also a key step in deciding whether two proofs of a sequent are the “same” in some suitable sense. In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \vdash B
            is provable or whether the function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \multimap B
            is inhabited (has a proof or a term witnessing that fact), but also the nature of the space of such proofs. Since any proof has a trivial cut-free formulation in a system where all provable sequents in the original system are simply postulated as axioms, a cut-elimination result worthy of the name will not merely replace a proof with one which is cut-free, but with a cut-free proof which is
            %em equivalent
            to the original. This idea is used for instance in proving
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorems"} coherence theorems
          %p
            Cut-elimination may also be used to give independent proof-theoretic motivation of the definition of a category, and other basic category theoretic notions, eg.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Dos99"} Došen 99
          %h2#connection_to_identities Connection to identities
          %p
            In the analogy between the composition and the cut rule, the analogue of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
            (or nullary compositions) is the identity rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \vdash A
          %p
            Typically, a cut-elimination algorithm goes hand-in-hand with an algorithm which eliminates the identity rule, or rather which pushes back identities as far as possible, down to identities for basic propositional variables (so for example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi q
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \wedge q \vdash p \wedge q
            may be proved using
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \vdash p
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \vdash q
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \vdash p
            itself must be adopted as an axiom).
          %p
            In fact, there is a sense in which elimination of cuts is seen as
            %em dual
            to elimination of identities, analogous to the sense in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+reduction"} beta reduction
            is seen as dual to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta+expansion"} eta expansion
          %h2#examples_of_elimination_steps Examples of elimination steps
          %p The conversion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊸
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi Λ
                            %mn 1
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi Λ
                            %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊸
                          %mi B
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi Λ
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi Λ
                            %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi Λ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi Λ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo →
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi Λ
                            %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %msub
                            %mi Λ
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Δ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi Λ
                        %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi Λ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi Λ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\displaystyle \frac{\Gamma, A \vdash B, \Delta}{\Gamma \vdash A \multimap B,\Delta} \;\;\; \frac{\Pi_1 \vdash A,\Lambda_1 \;\;\; \Pi_2,B \vdash \Lambda_2}{\Pi_2,\Pi_1, A\multimap B \vdash \Lambda_2,\Lambda_1}}{\Pi_2,\Pi_1, \Gamma \vdash \Lambda_2,\Lambda_1, \Delta}
                  \\quad\to\quad
                  \\frac{\displaystyle \frac{\Gamma, A \vdash B, \Delta \;\;\; \Pi_1 \vdash A,\Lambda_1}{\Pi_1,\Gamma \vdash \Lambda_1,B,\Delta} \;\;\; \Pi_2,B \vdash \Lambda_2}{\Pi_2,\Pi_1, \Gamma \vdash \Lambda_2, \Lambda_1,\Delta}
          %p
            replaces a single cut on the formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \multimap B
            with a pair of cuts on the formulas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\multimap}R
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\multimap}L
          %p Although this step replaces one cut by two, the cuts have been in effect pushed up the proof tree, to formulas of lower complexity. Cuts are finally eliminated when they have been pushed all the way up to identity axioms on propositional variables, by applying conversions of type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi axiom
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi axiom
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi cut
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo →
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi axiom
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\displaystyle \frac{}{x \vdash x}\; axiom \;\;\;\; \frac{}{x \vdash x}\; axiom}{x \vdash x} \; cut \quad\to\quad \frac{}{x \vdash x}\; axiom.
          %p Likewise, the conversion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo →
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi B
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mi B
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi B
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \wedge B \vdash A \wedge B
                  \\quad\to\quad
                  \\frac { \displaystyle \frac { A \vdash A } { A \wedge B \vdash A } \;\;\; \frac { B \vdash B } { A \wedge B \vdash B} } { A \wedge B \vdash A \wedge B }
          %p
            reconstructs the identity on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \wedge B
            from identities on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\wedge}R
            rule followed by the two
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\wedge}L
            rules (reading the derivation on the right bottom-up).
          %p
            (Compare these two conversions arising from cut- and identity-elimination to the lambda calculus conversions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.t_1) t_2 \to t_1[t_2/x]
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \to \langle\pi_1t,\pi_2 t\rangle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            reduction and an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            expansion respectively.)
          %h2#alternative_forms_of_the_cut_rule Alternative forms of the cut rule
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            (for instance), one sometimes sees sequents written in one-sided form:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \; \vdash \Gamma.
          %p
            Here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            operator is used to mediate between classical two-sided sequents and one-sided sequents, according to a scheme where a sequent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, A \vdash \Delta
            is associated with a sequent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta, \neg A
            (each being derivable from the other). Thus one can contemplate sequents where all formulae have been pushed to the right of the entailment symbol
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
          %p For such one-sided sequents, say in multiplicative linear logic, the cut rule may be expressed in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Δ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi cut
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\vdash \Gamma, \neg A \;\;\; \vdash \Delta, A}{\vdash \Gamma, \Delta} \; cut
          %p and this rule is ‘dual’ to one which introduces an identity:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi identity
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{}{\vdash \neg A, A} \; identity.
          %p
            Categorically, the cut rule in this form corresponds to the arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg A \otimes A \to \bot
            that implements an evaluation, and the identity rule corresponds to an arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo →
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \to \neg A
            %code ⅋
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = A \multimap A
            that names an identity morphism. These two arrows are de Morgan dual to one another.
          %h2#cut_elimination_as_a_computation Cut elimination as a computation
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"}> Gerhard Gentzen
            ‘s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gentzen%27s+Hauptsatz"} Hauptsatz
            is often formulated under the form: “The cut rule is an
            = succeed "\u201D-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rule"} admissible rule
          %p
            The theorem of Gentzen was formulated for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            and is also true in the other logics with a cut rule such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
          %ul
            %li
              Entry: proof
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{1}
              of a sequent
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            %li
              Output: proof
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{2}
              of the sequent
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
              which doesn’t use the cut rule“
          %p
            A better way to present things would be: “Here is an algorithm which takes in entry a proof
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{1}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            and returns a proof
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{2}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            which doesn’t use the cut rule”, because an actual algorithm is actually given and it is important to know what is this algorithm, we’re not interested only by its existence.
          %p The best way to tell it would be in fact as:
          %p
            “Here is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rewriting+system"} rewriting system
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rightarrow
            on the set of proofs of this logic such that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{1} \rightarrow \pi_{2}
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{1}, \pi_{2}
            are proofs of the same sequent, and for every proof
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{0}, \pi_{1}, \ldots, \pi_{n}
            of proofs of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi = \pi_{0} \rightarrow \pi_{1} \rightarrow ... \rightarrow \pi_{n}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{n}
            doesn’t use the cut rule.”
          %p
            One can after this study the properties of this particular rewriting system: is it
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confluent"} confluent
            = succeed "?" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                normalizing
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/normalizing"} ?
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+normalizing"} strongly normalizing
            %span.newWikiWord
              deterministic algorithm
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/deterministic+algorithm"} ?
            or a
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nondeterministic+algorithm"} nondeterministic algorithm
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+complexity"} computational complexity
            of the algorithms it provides to eliminate cuts?
          %p
            Unfortunately, the algorithmic aspect of cut elimination is rarely presented as explicitly, and most of the time the cut elimination is presented as the admissibility of the cut rule and the concrete algorithm to eliminate the instance of cuts in a proof
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            must be extracted by the reader from this proof (of the cut elimination) which nevertheless use it in order to do the proof (of the cut elimination).
          %p
            We’re not sure whether there isn’t such an algorithm of cut elimination for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            and this an ongoing subject of research. We can think that the usual sequent calculus of classical logic must be modified by using ideas such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarity+in+type+theory"} polarization
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/focusing"} focusing
            in order to understand the computational content of the cut elimination of classical logic.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of the cut rule as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+inference+rule"} structural inference rule
            originates (under the German name
            = succeed ")" do
              %em Schnitt
          %ul
            %li#Gentzen35
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"}> Gerhard Gentzen
                , §1.2.1 in:
                %em Untersuchungen über das logische Schließen I
                %em Mathematische Zeitschrift
                %strong 39
                1 (1935) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01201353"}> doi:10.1007/BF01201353
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Gentzen69
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"}> Gerhard Gentzen
                , §1.21 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Investigations into Logical Deduction
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Collected Papers of Gerhard Gentzen
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 55
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/bookseries/studies-in-logic-and-the-foundations-of-mathematics/vol/55"} ISBN:978-0-444-53419-4
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://logic-teaching.github.io/prop/texts/Gentzen%201969%20-%20Investigations%20into%20Logical%20Deduction.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cut-elimination_theorem"} Cut-elimination theorem
            %li#Dos99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kosta+Do%C5%A1en"}> Kosta Došen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cut Elimination in Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 6
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-017-1207-1"} doi:10.1007/978-94-017-1207-1
